 "drivingQuestion": "How can a punctured earthenware vessel still be a 'vessel' to be broken?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "small-root puncture purifies yet leaves it a vessel",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "zevachim.95a.2",
   "daf": "95a",
   "sentenceId": 1,
   "he": "כְּלִי חֶרֶס שֶׁיָּצָא כּוּ׳. ״כְּלִי״ אָמַר רַחֲמָנָא, וְלֹא כְּלִי הוּא! שֶׁנִּיקַּב בְּשׁוֹרֶשׁ קָטָן.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: With regard to «an earthenware vessel» in which a sin offering was cooked «that went outside» the curtains and became ritually impure outside the curtains, one punctures the vessel to render it ritually pure, brings the vessel back into the courtyard, and breaks it there. The Gemara asks: Why is there a need to break the earthenware vessel after puncturing it? «The Merciful One states:» “The earthenware «vessel…»shall be broken” (Leviticus 6:21), «and,» once it is punctured, «it is not a vessel.» The Gemara explains: «When it is punctured» with a hole only the size «of a small root,» the earthenware vessel is purified from the ritual impurity it contracted, but it remains a vessel for other purposes, such as holding fruit."
  }
 ],
